diff --git a/codex-rs/app-server/README.md b/codex-rs/app-server/README.md
index d64c230..8e86d55 100644
--- a/codex-rs/app-server/README.md
+++ b/codex-rs/app-server/README.md
@@ -113,3 +113,30 @@ Attachments record the resources currently associated with a thread, independent
 `thread/attachment/list` accepts one `threadId` and returns at most 100 attachments per page, ordered by creation time and attachment id. Continue with `nextCursor` and the same `threadId` until the cursor is `null`. Each thread can retain up to 100 attachments. Removing an attachment frees a slot for a new attachment.
 
 Attachment creation and deletion requests using the same thread ID are serialized across connections. The requesting client receives its response before the compact update is broadcast, and duplicate creates or absent deletes do not emit updates. Deleting the owning thread removes its attachments under the same lifecycle exclusion; queued attachment mutations then report that the thread was not found.
+
+### Mira conversation handoff
+
+The Mira runtime adds experimental `mira/thread/unload` with `threadId` (the root)
+and `threadIds` (its complete current conversation tree, at most 4096 IDs). The
+Mira broker holds a tree execution gate while calling it and until the new route
+commits. The runtime checks the whole scope for active work before unloading,
+waits for shutdown, listener drainage and durable ThreadStore flush, and replies
+with the confirmed `threadIds`. Absent threads are acknowledged for retry. Busy
+or incomplete teardown fails without authorizing a new writer. Other loaded
+conversations and the account process remain running. This operation does not
+archive threads, close durable graph edges, rewrite history or retry tools.
+
+### Mira memory residency
+
+Experimental `mira/thread/residency` is a local Node control RPC, blocked by the
+Mira broker and account tunnels. Each successful control request renews a 60-second
+lease that defers the normal idle timeout. Without renewal, upstream timeout
+behavior resumes. It does not subscribe, hydrate history, or alter explicit unload.
+The response contains `oldest` (an eligible leaf with `threadId`, opaque `revision`,
+and Unix-second `idleAt`) and `evictionScheduled`. Supplying that candidate as
+`evict` rechecks eligibility before scheduling ordinary native shutdown. This is
+not a durability acknowledgement; account handoff must use `mira/thread/unload`.
+Eligibility protects loaded families with active work or subscribers. Candidate
+revisions change when reused or replaced, so stale requests cannot evict a new
+idle period or replacement runtime. The Node shares an RSS budget across accounts
+and paces eviction, while the runtime retains authority over its lifecycle.
